type Topic={title:string; intro:string; blocks:{heading:string;text:string;example?:string}[]; takeaway:string; question:string; answer:string; options:string[]; explanation:string};
type Chapter={id:string;title:string;eyebrow:string;icon:string;accent:string;summary:string;topics:Topic[]};
const chapters:Chapter[]=[
{id:'foundations',title:'The Psychology of Mind & Behaviour',eyebrow:'01 · FOUNDATIONS',icon:'🧠',accent:'#f5a65b',summary:'Discover what psychology studies, how behaviour differs from mental processes, and the four goals that guide psychological inquiry.',topics:[
{title:'Defining psychology',intro:'Psychology is the scientific study of behaviour and mental processes. It examines how individuals and groups think, feel and act.',blocks:[{heading:'A broad field',text:'Psychology considers biological, psychological and social influences. Its scope ranges from the firing of a single neuron to the behaviour of a crowd.'},{heading:'Behaviour',text:'Behaviour includes outward, overt actions and reactions that can be observed and measured, such as talking, facial expressions, gestures and movement. It also includes internal states—feelings, attitudes, thoughts and emotions—which are inferred indirectly through tone of voice, physiological arousal or reactions to tasks.'},{heading:'Mental or cognitive processes',text:'These are internal operations of the mind, including memory, thinking, problem-solving, decision-making, language, perception and reasoning. They cannot be observed directly and are studied through tasks, self-report and brain-imaging technology.'}],takeaway:'Psychology studies both what people do and the mental processes that help explain why they do it.',question:'Which statement best defines psychology?',options:['The study of only observable actions','The scientific study of behaviour and mental processes','The study of the brain alone','The study of mental illness only'],answer:'The scientific study of behaviour and mental processes',explanation:'Psychology includes observable behaviour and internal mental processes, while considering biological, psychological and social influences.'},
{title:'The four goals of psychology',intro:'The four connected goals are description, explanation, prediction and control. Together they move from observing behaviour to understanding and changing it.',blocks:[{heading:'01 · Description',text:'Accurately identify and record what is happening. Example: a survey reports how common exam anxiety is and how students experience it.'},{heading:'02 · Explanation',text:'Identify why a behaviour or event occurs. Example: investigate factors that contribute to exam anxiety.'},{heading:'03 · Prediction',text:'Use known relationships to anticipate what may happen. Example: use earlier experiences to estimate who may be at risk of later anxiety.'},{heading:'04 · Control',text:'Apply knowledge to influence or produce change. Applied and clinical psychology often focus on lasting behavioural change, such as a therapist helping a client change a pattern.'}],takeaway:'Description and prediction are common across research; control is especially important in applied and clinical work.',question:'A national survey measures how common exam anxiety is, without testing causes. Which goal is primary?',options:['Control','Prediction','Description','Explanation'],answer:'Description',explanation:'The survey records prevalence and experience. It does not explain causes, predict outcomes or attempt to change behaviour.'}
]},
{id:'behaviour',title:'Mapping Behaviour',eyebrow:'02 · CLASSIFICATION',icon:'🧭',accent:'#e47b69',summary:'Classify behaviour across four dimensions and understand why one action can belong to several categories at once.',topics:[
{title:'Four dimensions of behaviour',intro:'Behaviour can be classified along four dimensions. They are not mutually exclusive: a single act can be overt, unconscious and involuntary at the same time.',blocks:[{heading:'Overt vs covert',text:'Overt behaviour is directly observable, such as talking, walking and gesturing. Behaviourists such as Watson preferred observable behaviour because it can be measured objectively. Covert behaviour refers to hidden internal processes such as thinking and feeling; it is measured indirectly through self-report or physiological indicators.'},{heading:'Conscious vs unconscious',text:'Conscious behaviour occurs with awareness and deliberation, such as studying or deliberately recalling a memory. Unconscious behaviour occurs outside awareness. Freud emphasised repressed urges and memories; automatic processes such as typing or face recognition can also happen without conscious effort.'},{heading:'Rational vs irrational',text:'Rational behaviour involves logical, realistic weighing of costs and benefits. Irrational behaviour may be driven by emotion or distorted thinking, such as overspending or phobias. Cognitive biases matter, and CBT targets cognitive distortions. Context must be considered before labelling behaviour irrational.'},{heading:'Voluntary vs involuntary',text:'Voluntary behaviour is intentional and controlled, such as speaking or raising a hand. Involuntary behaviour is automatic, such as blinking, reflexes and heart rate. Skinner’s operant conditioning explains learned voluntary behaviour; Rollo May’s existential view emphasises purposeful choice rather than purely reactive behaviour.'}],takeaway:'Always classify an action across all four dimensions and consider context before drawing conclusions.',question:'A person automatically bites their nails while anxious. Which combination best fits the example in the PDF?',options:['Covert, conscious, voluntary, rational','Overt, unconscious, involuntary, potentially irrational','Covert, unconscious, voluntary, rational','Overt, conscious, voluntary, rational'],answer:'Overt, unconscious, involuntary, potentially irrational',explanation:'Nail biting is visible (overt), often outside awareness (unconscious), not deliberately controlled (involuntary), and may not serve a realistic cost-benefit purpose.'},
{title:'Why classification matters',intro:'The dimensions connect to major debates and practical decisions in psychology.',blocks:[{heading:'Theoretical debates',text:'These dimensions map onto behaviourism versus psychoanalysis, free will versus determinism, and debates about rational thought and behaviour change.'},{heading:'Worked example: nail biting while anxious',text:'The act is overt because it can be seen; often unconscious because the person may not notice it; involuntary because it is not deliberately controlled; and potentially irrational if it serves no realistic cost-benefit purpose.'},{heading:'Clinical implications',text:'Unconscious or involuntary behaviours may be addressed through habit reversal training. Irrational behaviours may be addressed through cognitive restructuring. Accurate classification helps guide the choice of intervention.'}],takeaway:'Classification is not only theoretical—it can guide assessment and intervention.',question:'Which intervention is linked in the PDF to irrational behaviour?',options:['Habit reversal training','Cognitive restructuring','Random assignment','Debriefing'],answer:'Cognitive restructuring',explanation:'The PDF links irrational behaviour and cognitive distortions with cognitive restructuring, while habit reversal training is linked to unconscious or involuntary habits.'}
]},
{id:'science',title:'Psychology as a Science',eyebrow:'03 · SCIENTIFIC METHOD',icon:'🔬',accent:'#72b8b0',summary:'Trace psychology’s scientific history, core characteristics, research methods and ethical responsibilities.',topics:[
{title:'Historical journey & characteristics',intro:'Psychology has a long philosophical past but a comparatively short history as an empirical science.',blocks:[{heading:'A short historical journey',text:'Hermann Ebbinghaus described psychology as having a “long past, short history.” Gustav Fechner developed psychophysics, relating physical stimuli to sensation. Wilhelm Wundt established the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879 and used objective introspection. Ebbinghaus conducted experimental research on memory. John B. Watson became a founder of Behaviourism, restricting psychology to observable behaviour.'},{heading:'Empirical observation',text:'Scientific claims must be testable and evidence-based.'},{heading:'Systematic theory',text:'Data are organised into explanations that can account for patterns and generate further questions.'},{heading:'Measurement and operational definitions',text:'Measurement assigns numbers according to agreed rules. Operational definitions specify exactly how a concept is measured, allowing comparison across studies.'},{heading:'Replication',text:'Repeatable procedures and findings support reliability.'}],takeaway:'Psychology’s scientific status depends on evidence, systematic methods, clear measurement and replication.',question:'Where and when did Wundt establish the first psychology laboratory?',options:['Vienna, 1890','Leipzig, 1879','London, 1905','Calcutta, 1916'],answer:'Leipzig, 1879',explanation:'Wundt’s laboratory at Leipzig in 1879 is identified in the PDF as the birth of scientific psychology.'},
{title:'Methods of scientific inquiry',intro:'Psychologists use descriptive, correlational and experimental approaches. The method must match the research question.',blocks:[{heading:'Descriptive methods',text:'Naturalistic observation, laboratory observation, case studies and surveys describe behaviour or experience. They do not by themselves establish cause.'},{heading:'Correlational methods',text:'Correlation describes the direction and strength of a statistical relationship between variables. Correlation does not establish causation.'},{heading:'Experiments',text:'Experiments are the method that can demonstrate cause and effect when properly designed. They use independent and dependent variables, experimental and control groups, and random assignment.'},{heading:'Reducing bias',text:'Placebo effects and experimenter effects can be addressed through single-blind or double-blind designs.'}],takeaway:'Correlation is not causation; controlled experiments are used to test causal relationships.',question:'Which research method can establish a cause-and-effect relationship?',options:['Survey','Case study','Correlational study','Experiment'],answer:'Experiment',explanation:'A controlled experiment manipulates an independent variable and measures its effect on a dependent variable.'},
{title:'Research ethics',intro:'Ethical safeguards protect participants and support responsible research.',blocks:[{heading:'Human participants',text:'Key considerations include informed consent, minimising deception, the right to withdraw, protection from harm, debriefing, confidentiality and correcting undesirable effects.'},{heading:'Animal research',text:'Researchers should avoid unnecessary pain or suffering.'}],takeaway:'Scientific value never removes the responsibility to protect participants.',question:'Which is an ethical safeguard in psychological research?',options:['Preventing participants from withdrawing','Avoiding debriefing','Informed consent','Increasing unnecessary deception'],answer:'Informed consent',explanation:'Informed consent is a core safeguard, alongside the right to withdraw, confidentiality and protection from harm.'}
]},
{id:'perspectives',title:'Natural Science & Social Science',eyebrow:'04 · TWO LENSES',icon:'⚖️',accent:'#a78bd4',summary:'Compare biological and contextual explanations, their branches, strengths and limitations.',topics:[
{title:'Psychology as a natural science',intro:'This view studies humans as biological organisms, using experimental logic, controlled measurement and causal regularities.',blocks:[{heading:'The case for a natural-science view',text:'Behaviour is rooted in the brain, nervous system, hormones and genetics. Researchers study reaction times, sensory thresholds, neural activity and physiological arousal. Wundt applied precise measurement to inner experience; Behaviourism focused on observable and measurable behaviour.'},{heading:'Branches',text:'Biopsychology studies biological bases of behaviour, including the brain, nervous system, genetics, hormones and disease. Sensation and Perception examines sense organs and mechanisms behind perception, including illusions, depth and movement. Comparative Psychology compares behaviour across species. Experimental Psychology investigates learning, memory, thinking, sensation, perception, motivation and emotion, primarily through controlled laboratory methods.'},{heading:'Behaviourism as a model case',text:'Behaviourism uses controlled experiments, operational definitions for concepts such as reinforcement, punishment and stimulus, replicable findings and predictive aims. Skinner’s operant conditioning manipulated reinforcement schedules and measured response rates.'},{heading:'Limitations',text:'Oversimplification excludes thoughts, emotions and motivations. Reductionism can reduce behaviour to simple stimulus-response relationships, risking an incomplete account of human experience.'}],takeaway:'Natural-science methods offer precision and control, but a purely biological or observable account can be too narrow.',question:'Which branch compares behaviour across species?',options:['Social Psychology','Comparative Psychology','Consumer Psychology','Community Psychology'],answer:'Comparative Psychology',explanation:'Comparative Psychology compares behaviour across species to generate insights relevant to human behaviour.'},
{title:'Critiques: McDougall & Dilthey',intro:'Critics argue that human experience cannot be captured fully through objective measurement alone.',blocks:[{heading:'McDougall’s critique',text:'An overly objective approach can overlook inner experience, such as daydreams and fantasies, when only observable behaviour counts as data. Strict determinism can also overlook agency, free will and moral responsibility.'},{heading:'Dilthey’s distinction',text:'Naturwissenschaften refers to natural sciences, which study external physical phenomena through observation and causal explanation. Geisteswissenschaften refers to human sciences, which study human experience through a different method.'},{heading:'Verstehen and Erklären',text:'Verstehen means interpretative, empathetic understanding situated in historical and psychological context. Erklären means explaining through external causal relationships. Dilthey argued that understanding grief, for example, requires personal, historical and cultural context, not only a mechanical account.'}],takeaway:'Understanding human meaning may require interpretation as well as causal explanation.',question:'What does Dilthey mean by Verstehen?',options:['A controlled experiment','An interpretative, empathetic understanding','A statistical correlation','A biological reflex'],answer:'An interpretative, empathetic understanding',explanation:'Verstehen means understanding experience from within its personal, historical and psychological context.'},
{title:'Psychology as a social science',intro:'The social-science view understands people as social beings whose behaviour is shaped by relationships, groups, culture and institutions.',blocks:[{heading:'Key branches',text:'Social Psychology studies attitudes, conformity, persuasion, prejudice, friendship and aggression. Cultural Psychology examines culture, subculture, ethnicity and cross-cultural patterns. Community Psychology focuses on social problems, adaptation to work and living groups, and community mental health. Consumer Psychology studies packaging, advertising, marketing and psychological traits shaping consumer behaviour.'},{heading:'Sociocultural perspective',text:'Human nature is contextual and socially shaped rather than fixed or purely internal. It is one of the major modern frameworks alongside psychodynamic, behavioural, humanistic, cognitive, biopsychological and evolutionary perspectives.'},{heading:'Vygotsky’s contribution',text:'Lev Vygotsky argued that consciousness is shaped by socialisation. Social interaction and language are central to cognitive development. Key concepts include collaborative learning, scaffolding and the zone of proximal development.'},{heading:'Cross-cultural and indigenous psychology',text:'Cross-cultural research compares behaviour across cultures to examine environment and heredity. Indigenisation recognises that Western theories may not fit non-Western contexts. In India, culturally grounded psychology may consider karma, dharma and joint-family structures, and use ethnographic and qualitative methods.'},{heading:'Limitations',text:'A purely social account can neglect biological foundations, individual temperament differences within a culture and universal biological changes such as puberty and ageing.'}],takeaway:'Culture and context matter, but they do not erase biology or individual differences.',question:'Which is a key concept associated with Vygotsky?',options:['Random assignment','Zone of proximal development','Double-blind design','Psychophysics'],answer:'Zone of proximal development',explanation:'The PDF lists collaborative learning, scaffolding and the zone of proximal development among concepts arising from Vygotsky’s work.'}
]},
{id:'hybrid',title:'The Biopsychosocial Bridge',eyebrow:'05 · INTEGRATION',icon:'🌐',accent:'#80a7e8',summary:'Bring biological, psychological and social explanations together to understand behaviour and mental health.',topics:[
{title:'Psychology as a hybrid discipline',intro:'Psychology studies a biological organism embedded in a social and cultural world. It therefore draws on both natural-science and social-science approaches.',blocks:[{heading:'The biopsychosocial model',text:'Behaviour and mental health are understood through three interacting factors: Biological—genetics, brain chemistry and physiology; Psychological—thoughts, emotions, coping strategies and learning history; Social—relationships, culture, socioeconomic context and social support.'},{heading:'Example: stress',text:'Biological: HPA-axis activation and cortisol release. Psychological: individual appraisal, thinking patterns and coping resources. Social: support from family, friends and the workplace, plus cultural attitudes toward achievement and failure.'},{heading:'Example: depression',text:'A complete account may consider neurotransmitter imbalance (biological), negative thinking patterns (psychological), and isolation, poverty or stigma (social). No single level is sufficient alone.'},{heading:'Psychology at the crossroads',text:'Psychology borrows experimental rigour, laboratory methods and biological grounding from natural sciences, while drawing on interpretive, contextual and culturally sensitive methods from social sciences and humanities.'}],takeaway:'A complete explanation considers interacting biological, psychological and social influences.',question:'Which combination best represents all three levels of the biopsychosocial model in a stress intervention?',options:['Cortisol levels, negative thinking patterns, workplace social support','Brain chemistry, HPA activation, cognitive appraisal only','Coping resources, family relationships, socioeconomic status only','Genetics, culture and peer groups only'],answer:'Cortisol levels, negative thinking patterns, workplace social support',explanation:'This option includes one biological, one psychological and one social factor, matching the model’s three levels.'}
]},
{id:'discipline',title:'Psychology as a Discipline',eyebrow:'06 · THE FIELD',icon:'🏛️',accent:'#d4a0c8',summary:'Explore basic and applied psychology, modern perspectives, professional infrastructure and training pathways.',topics:[
{title:'Basic and applied psychology',intro:'Psychology has two broad branches: basic psychology and applied psychology.',blocks:[{heading:'Basic psychology',text:'The academic branch develops theory and conducts research to expand knowledge about behaviour and mental processes.'},{heading:'Applied psychology',text:'The applied branch uses psychological knowledge to address real-world problems. Clinical and applied work often emphasises behavioural change and practical outcomes.'}],takeaway:'Basic psychology builds knowledge; applied psychology uses it to address practical problems.',question:'Which branch primarily focuses on theory and research?',options:['Applied psychology','Basic psychology','Clinical registration','Community intervention'],answer:'Basic psychology',explanation:'The PDF describes basic psychology as academic, focused on theory and research.'},
{title:'Modern perspectives',intro:'Contemporary psychology is organised around several overlapping theoretical perspectives.',blocks:[{heading:'Psychodynamic',text:'Focuses on the unconscious mind, childhood experience and the development of the self.'},{heading:'Behavioural',text:'Views behaviour as learned through classical and operant conditioning.'},{heading:'Humanistic',text:'Known as the “third force” in psychology; emphasises human potential, free will and self-actualisation.'},{heading:'Evolutionary',text:'Explains mental traits as adaptations shaped by natural selection.'},{heading:'Sociocultural',text:'Emphasises how context, culture and language shape development.'},{heading:'Interdisciplinary practice',text:'Perspectives overlap. Clinicians may draw on multiple perspectives. Psychology also draws on biology, medicine, sociology, anthropology, philosophy, computer science and education.'}],takeaway:'Modern psychologists often integrate perspectives rather than follow one approach exclusively.',question:'Which perspective emphasises human potential, free will and self-actualisation?',options:['Behavioural','Psychodynamic','Humanistic','Evolutionary'],answer:'Humanistic',explanation:'Humanistic psychology is described as the “third force” and emphasises human potential, free will and self-actualisation.'},
{title:'Professional infrastructure & training',intro:'Psychology is sustained by institutions, professional communities, journals and structured training.',blocks:[{heading:'Indian landmarks and institutions',text:'Indian Science Congress (1923); Indian Psychologists Association (1924/25); Indian Journal of Psychology (1926); All India Institute of Mental Health (AIIMH), now NIMHANS (1955); University Grants Commission (UGC, 1956). The PDF also notes NIMHANS in Bengaluru (1974) in its institutional timeline.'},{heading:'Training pathways',text:'Undergraduate study provides broad exposure to branches of psychology. Postgraduate study allows specialisation in a basic or applied branch. Clinical and counselling pathways require supervised clinical training and professional registration. Research careers generally require a doctoral degree and may lead to research and teaching.'},{heading:'Why infrastructure matters',text:'This layered system supports shared foundational knowledge, peer review, quality control and the incorporation of new advances into the field.'}],takeaway:'Psychology is an organised, interdisciplinary profession—not only a collection of theories.',question:'According to the PDF, which pathway generally requires a doctoral degree?',options:['Undergraduate exposure','Research branches','Basic introductory study','A single survey'],answer:'Research branches',explanation:'The PDF states that research branches require a doctoral degree and can lead to research and teaching careers.'}
]},
{id:'history',title:'Historical Landmarks',eyebrow:'07 · TIMELINES',icon:'⌛',accent:'#e4b75f',summary:'Review key Western and Indian milestones, institutional development and classification systems shown in the PDF.',topics:[
{title:'Western timeline',intro:'The PDF presents milestones in the emergence of scientific psychology, schools of thought, diagnostic manuals and notable Nobel-linked work.',blocks:[{heading:'Founders and early milestones',text:'1878: G. Stanley Hall—first PhD in psychology. 1879: Wilhelm Wundt’s first psychology laboratory at Leipzig. 1892: Hall becomes first APA president. 1894: Margaret Floy Washburn—first woman PhD in psychology. 1905: Mary Whiton Calkins—first woman APA president.'},{heading:'Schools of thought',text:'The timeline places Structuralism around 1879, Functionalism in the 1890s, Psychoanalysis around 1900, Gestalt around 1912, Behaviourism around 1913, Humanistic psychology in the 1950s and Cognitive psychology in the 1950s–60s. Positive Psychology is marked at 1998.'},{heading:'Diagnostic classification timelines',text:'DSM: DSM-III (1980; turning point, symptom-based), DSM-III-R (1987), DSM-IV (1994), DSM-IV-TR (2000) and DSM-5-TR (2022). ICD: ICD-6 (1949; first mental-disorders section), ICD-9 (1978), ICD-10 (1990–1994) and ICD-11 (2019–2022).'},{heading:'Nobel highlights shown',text:'The graphic lists Pavlov (1904), Egas Moniz (1949; lobotomy, noted as controversial), Sperry (1981; split-brain) and Kahneman (2002).'}],takeaway:'Use the dates as a timeline: laboratory psychology (1879), major schools, then evolving diagnostic systems.',question:'Which DSM edition is marked as a symptom-based turning point in the PDF?',options:['DSM-I','DSM-III','DSM-IV-TR','DSM-5-TR'],answer:'DSM-III',explanation:'The timeline labels DSM-III (1980) as a turning point and symptom-based.'},
{title:'Eastern and Indian timeline',intro:'The PDF traces Indian psychology from ancient intellectual roots to formal institutions, expansion and indigenisation.',blocks:[{heading:'Ancient roots',text:'The graphic identifies the Vedas, Upanishads, Buddha and Patanjali’s Yoga Sutras as ancient roots of Indian psychology.'},{heading:'Pre-independence adaptation phase',text:'Calcutta laboratory (1905); Department of Psychology at the University of Calcutta (1916, associated with Sen Gupta); Indian Psychoanalytic Society (1922, Bose); Indian Psychological Association (1925). The PDF explicitly notes that the Calcutta laboratory (1905) came after Wundt’s Leipzig laboratory (1879).'}, {heading:'Expansion and indigenisation',text:'The expansion phase involved growth of psychology education, research and practice in India. The indigenisation phase, led by Durganand Sinha, focused on developing indigenous perspectives and theories.'},{heading:'Formal recognition and institutions',text:'The Pondicherry Manifesto (2002) is shown as formal recognition of Indian psychology as a distinct discipline. The timeline lists AIIMH Bengaluru (1954) and NIMHANS (1974).'}],takeaway:'Indian psychology combines ancient intellectual traditions with modern institutions and culturally grounded theory.',question:'Which event is shown as formal recognition of Indian psychology as a distinct discipline?',options:['Calcutta laboratory, 1905','Indian Psychological Association, 1925','Pondicherry Manifesto, 2002','Wundt’s laboratory, 1879'],answer:'Pondicherry Manifesto, 2002',explanation:'The PDF identifies the Pondicherry Manifesto (2002) as formal recognition of Indian psychology as a distinct discipline.'}
]},

{id:'branches',title:'Branches & Careers in Psychology',eyebrow:'08 · SPECIALISATIONS',icon:'🧩',accent:'#6da6a1',summary:'Explore the major branches of psychology, where they are practised and how psychological knowledge appears across professions.',topics:[
{title:'Major branches of psychology',intro:'Psychology is a broad family of specialised branches. Each branch focuses on a particular aspect of behaviour or applies psychological knowledge to a real-world setting.',blocks:[{heading:'Clinical and counselling psychology',text:'Studies the diagnosis, causes and treatment of disorders and emotional difficulties through psychotherapy and assessment. Clinical psychologists use approaches such as psychodynamic, cognitive-behavioural and humanistic methods. The PDF distinguishes psychologists from psychiatrists: psychologists do not prescribe medication, whereas psychiatrists are medical doctors who can.'},{heading:'Work, education and social life',text:'Industrial/Organizational Psychology studies workplace behaviour, employee wellbeing, productivity, selection, training, motivation, leadership, job satisfaction and organisational structure. Educational Psychology studies classroom dynamics, teaching styles, learning, educational tests, curricula, motivation and memory. Social Psychology studies attitudes, conformity, obedience, persuasion, prejudice and aggression.'},{heading:'Development and health',text:'Developmental Psychology studies change across the lifespan, from infancy to old age, including child, adolescent and gerontological psychology. Health Psychology studies psychological influences on physical health and illness, health-risk behaviours, stress effects on immune and cardiovascular systems, and treatment adherence.'},{heading:'Law, defence and sport',text:'Forensic Psychology applies psychology within the justice system, including competency to stand trial, risk of reoffending, mental state at offence, eyewitness testimony, jury selection and offender rehabilitation. Military Psychology studies personnel selection, leadership, resilience and PTSD support. Sports Psychology examines motivation, concentration, performance anxiety and team cohesion; techniques include visualisation and self-talk.'},{heading:'Brain, environment and wellbeing',text:'Neuropsychology studies relationships between brain and behaviour, including effects of injury, disease and degeneration on memory, language and personality, using assessment and brain imaging such as MRI and EEG. Environmental Psychology examines how noise, crowding, natural light and green spaces affect mood and cognition. Positive Psychology scientifically studies strengths, virtues and human flourishing, including wellbeing, resilience, gratitude, flow and meaning. Spiritual Psychology examines spirituality, religious belief and psychological wellbeing, drawing on Western transpersonal psychology and Eastern traditions such as Yoga and Vedanta.'}],takeaway:'The branch selected depends on the question being studied and the setting in which psychological knowledge is applied.',question:'Which branch studies the effects of noise, crowding and green space on mood and cognition?',options:['Forensic Psychology','Environmental Psychology','Neuropsychology','Military Psychology'],answer:'Environmental Psychology',explanation:'Environmental Psychology examines interactions between people and their physical surroundings.'},
{title:'Professions that use psychology',intro:'Psychology is relevant to professions involving understanding, communicating with, influencing or supporting people.',blocks:[{heading:'Organisations, education and justice',text:'HR professionals use psychology in selection, performance management and conflict resolution. Industrial/Organizational psychologists may develop selection tests and investigate low morale. Teachers use memory, attention, learning theory and behaviour management. Police use social and forensic psychology in interviewing and eyewitness reliability; lawyers use persuasion and group decision-making insights; probation officers apply behaviour-change principles.'},{heading:'Mental health roles',text:'Clinical and counselling psychologists assess and treat through therapy and testing but do not prescribe medication. Psychiatrists are medical doctors and can prescribe medication. Counsellors and psychotherapists provide talk-based support in settings such as schools, workplaces and private practice. School/educational psychologists assess learning difficulties, behavioural concerns and giftedness.'},{heading:'Technology, media, health and sport',text:'UX designers and HCI researchers apply cognitive and perceptual psychology to usability. Advertising professionals use consumer psychology for persuasion and decision-making; journalists apply insights about attention, framing and emotional response. Physicians and nurses use health psychology for communication and treatment adherence. Physiotherapists and occupational therapists apply motivation principles to rehabilitation. Coaches use motivation, focus and performance-anxiety research.'},{heading:'Emerging intersections',text:'Behavioural data analysts and economists apply judgement and bias principles to consumer choices. Conversational-AI designers use cognitive and social psychology to improve usability. Psychology is increasingly relevant to trust, transparency and human–AI interaction.'}],takeaway:'Psychological knowledge extends beyond therapy into education, organisations, justice, design, media, medicine and technology.',question:'According to the PDF, which profession can prescribe medication?',options:['Clinical psychologist','Psychiatrist','School psychologist','Counsellor'],answer:'Psychiatrist',explanation:'The PDF identifies psychiatrists as medical doctors who can prescribe medication; psychologists and counsellors rely on assessment and talk-based interventions.'}
]},
{id:'approaches',title:'Approaches & Forces in Psychology',eyebrow:'09 · THEORETICAL LENSES',icon:'🔭',accent:'#b48bd2',summary:'Compare the major schools and approaches that explain behaviour and mental processes, including their contributions and limitations.',topics:[
{title:'Early schools: Structuralism and Functionalism',intro:'Early schools asked different questions about conscious experience and the purpose of mental processes.',blocks:[{heading:'Structuralism — Edward B. Titchener',text:'Titchener, a student of Wilhelm Wundt, founded the first formal school of psychology. Structuralism focused on the structure of conscious experience and proposed that it could be broken into three basic elements: sensations, images and affections (feelings and emotions). Its main method was introspection, in which trained participants described conscious experiences under controlled conditions. Contribution: helped establish psychology as a laboratory-based scientific discipline. Limitation: introspection was subjective and inconsistent.'},{heading:'Functionalism — William James',text:'Functionalism emerged as the first American school and developed partly as a reaction to Structuralism. Rather than asking what the mind is made of, it asked what the mind does. Influenced by Darwin’s theory of evolution, it focused on the functions of consciousness and behaviour, adaptation to the environment and the practical value of mental processes. James described consciousness as a “stream of consciousness.” Functionalists used introspection, experiments, animal studies and applied research. Contribution: laid foundations for applied psychology and influenced Behaviourism and Evolutionary Psychology.'}],takeaway:'Structuralism examined the elements of conscious experience; Functionalism examined the purpose and adaptive value of mental processes.',question:'Who founded Functionalism?',options:['Edward B. Titchener','William James','Sigmund Freud','John B. Watson'],answer:'William James',explanation:'The PDF identifies William James as the founder of Functionalism, the first American school of psychology.'},
{title:'Psychodynamic and Neo-Freudian approaches',intro:'The psychodynamic approach emphasises unconscious influences on behaviour, while Neo-Freudians expanded the role of social and interpersonal experience.',blocks:[{heading:'Psychodynamic — Sigmund Freud',text:'Freud proposed that thoughts, emotions and behaviours are influenced by unconscious motives, conflicts and early childhood experiences. Key concepts include the unconscious mind, Id, Ego, Superego, defence mechanisms and psychosexual stages of development. Personality develops through interactions among mental structures and unresolved childhood conflicts. Clinical methods include free association and dream analysis. Contribution: transformed understanding of personality and psychotherapy and highlighted early experiences. Limitation: many ideas are difficult to test scientifically and rely on subjective interpretation.'},{heading:'Neo-Freudians',text:'Neo-Freudians accepted Freud’s emphasis on the unconscious but modified many of his ideas. They gave greater importance to social, cultural and interpersonal experiences, conscious choices, relationships and personal growth, rather than focusing mainly on biological instincts.'}],takeaway:'Psychodynamic theories highlight unconscious conflict and early experience; Neo-Freudians broadened the account to include relationships and culture.',question:'Which approach uses free association and dream analysis to uncover unconscious conflicts?',options:['Cognitive','Psychodynamic','Behaviourist','Evolutionary'],answer:'Psychodynamic',explanation:'The PDF lists free association and dream analysis as clinical techniques used in the psychodynamic approach.'},
{title:'Behaviourism and Humanistic psychology',intro:'Behaviourism focuses on learned observable actions; Humanistic psychology emphasises agency, growth and human potential.',blocks:[{heading:'Behaviourism',text:'The Behavioural Approach studies observable and measurable behaviour rather than thoughts or feelings. It argues that behaviour is learned through interaction with the environment and conditioning. Key concepts include classical conditioning (Pavlov), operant conditioning (Skinner), reinforcement, punishment and stimulus–response learning. Watson is associated with Behaviourism; Bandura is associated with observational learning and modelling. The ABC sequence is antecedent (trigger) → behaviour (action) → consequence (outcome), which can lead to reinforcement or punishment. Contribution: increased objectivity and scientific methods, with applications in education, therapy and behaviour modification. Limitation: may overlook thoughts, emotions and biological factors.'},{heading:'Humanistic — the third force',text:'Humanistic psychology emerged in opposition to Behaviourism and Psychoanalysis. It views human beings as inherently good, with capacity for free will, personal growth and self-development. It shifted psychology towards positive human potential and influenced counselling, education and Positive Psychology. Limitation: concepts such as self-actualisation are difficult to measure scientifically.'}],takeaway:'Behaviourism explains learning through conditioning and consequences; Humanistic psychology emphasises free will and personal growth.',question:'Which approach is described as the “third force” in psychology?',options:['Structuralism','Humanistic psychology','Behaviourism','Psychodynamic psychology'],answer:'Humanistic psychology',explanation:'The PDF explicitly describes Humanistic psychology as the third force, in opposition to Behaviourism and Psychoanalysis.'},
{title:'Cognitive and Sociocultural approaches',intro:'These approaches explain behaviour through information processing and the social-cultural context in which people develop.',blocks:[{heading:'Cognitive approach',text:'The Cognitive Approach views the mind as an information-processing system. It studies perception, thinking, memory, problem-solving and decision-making, arguing that internal processes influence behaviour beyond external stimuli. Key concepts include attention, perception, memory (encoding, storage and retrieval), thinking and problem-solving, information processing and schemas. Ulric Neisser is identified as a pioneer; Jean Piaget studied cognitive development; George A. Miller is associated with “The Magical Number.” It revived scientific study of the mind and influenced cognitive neuroscience and CBT. Limitation: may underestimate emotion, unconscious processes and social context.'},{heading:'Sociocultural approach',text:'The Sociocultural Approach explains behaviour through social interactions, culture and environment. Thoughts, emotions and behaviours are shaped by cultural values, social norms and relationships, and may vary across societies. Lev Vygotsky emphasised culture and social interaction in cognitive development and the Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD): tasks a child can perform with guidance but not yet alone. Albert Bandura’s Social Learning Theory highlights observational learning and modelling. Concepts include cultural context, social norms, socialisation, social learning and observational learning. Contribution: improves understanding of cultural diversity. Limitation: may underestimate biological and individual factors.'}],takeaway:'Cognitive psychology studies internal information processing; Sociocultural psychology studies how culture and interaction shape development and behaviour.',question:'The Zone of Proximal Development is associated with:',options:['Lev Vygotsky','Albert Bandura','George A. Miller','Charles Darwin'],answer:'Lev Vygotsky',explanation:'The PDF associates Vygotsky with the Zone of Proximal Development and the role of social interaction in cognitive development.'},
{title:'Biopsychological, Evolutionary and Eclectic approaches',intro:'These approaches explain behaviour through biological processes, adaptation across generations or an integration of multiple perspectives.',blocks:[{heading:'Biopsychological approach',text:'Explains behaviour through biological processes such as the brain, nervous system, hormones and genetics. It proposes that thoughts, emotions and behaviours have a biological basis and uses scientific methods such as brain imaging and neurophysiological research. Contribution: advanced understanding of biological bases of behaviour and mental disorders. Limitation: may overlook psychological, social and cultural influences.'},{heading:'Evolutionary approach',text:'Explains behaviour as a result of natural selection and adaptation. Behaviours and mental processes may have evolved because they increased survival and reproductive success. Key concepts include natural selection, adaptation, survival, reproduction and the evolution of behaviour. Charles Darwin is the key psychologist/scientist named in the PDF. Contribution: offers explanations for universal human behaviours and emotions. Limitation: some explanations are difficult to test directly and may overlook cultural influences.'},{heading:'Eclectic approach',text:'Combines ideas and techniques from multiple psychological approaches rather than relying on a single theory. It integrates biological, cognitive, behavioural, psychodynamic and humanistic perspectives, tailoring explanations and interventions to individual needs. It is widely used in counselling and psychotherapy. Contribution: comprehensive and practical understanding. Limitation: requires careful integration and may lack one unifying theoretical framework.'}],takeaway:'No single approach explains every aspect of behaviour; an eclectic approach combines perspectives according to the individual and problem.',question:'Which approach combines techniques from several psychological perspectives?',options:['Structuralism','Eclectic approach','Behaviourism','Evolutionary approach'],answer:'Eclectic approach',explanation:'The Eclectic Approach integrates multiple perspectives and tailors interventions to individual needs.'},
{title:'Seven forces of psychology',intro:'The PDF presents seven forces as major perspectives that explain human behaviour and mental processes.',blocks:[{heading:'The seven forces',text:'First Force: Psychoanalysis — unconscious motives, conflicts and early childhood experiences. Second Force: Behaviourism — observable behaviour and learning through conditioning. Third Force: Humanistic Psychology — free will, personal growth and self-actualisation. Fourth Force: Transpersonal Psychology — spirituality, consciousness and peak experiences. Fifth Force: Multicultural and Sociocultural Psychology — culture, society and diversity. Sixth Force: Positive Psychology — strengths, wellbeing and flourishing. Seventh Force: Indigenous and Integral Psychology — culturally rooted and holistic psychologies.'},{heading:'How to use the framework',text:'The forces build on or react to earlier approaches and offer broader ways to understand psychology. The list is a framework of perspectives, not a claim that one force replaces all others.'}],takeaway:'The seven forces broaden psychological explanation from unconscious processes and learning to culture, wellbeing, spirituality and indigenous perspectives.',question:'Which force focuses on strengths, wellbeing and flourishing?',options:['Second Force','Fourth Force','Sixth Force','Seventh Force'],answer:'Sixth Force',explanation:'The PDF identifies Positive Psychology as the sixth force, focused on strengths, wellbeing and flourishing.'}
]}

];

